<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-power-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Power Series</title>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x)=e^x</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a linear approximation <m>L_1(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p><m>L_1(x)</m> should satisfy <m>L_1(0)=f(0)</m> and <m>L_1^\prime(0)=f^\prime(0)</m></p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>L_1(x)=1+x</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a quadratic approximation <m>L_2(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p><m>L_2(x)</m> should satisfy 
          <ul>
            <li><m>L_2(0)=f(0)</m> </li>
            <li><m>L_2^\prime(0)=f^\prime(0)</m></li>
            <li><m>L_2^{\prime \prime}(0)=f^{\prime \prime}(0)</m></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>L_2(x)=1+x+\frac{1}{2}x^2</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a degree 3 approximation <m>L_3(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p><m>L_3(x)</m> should satisfy 
          <ul>
            <li><m>L_3(0)=f(0)</m> </li>
            <li><m>L_3^\prime(0)=f^\prime(0)</m></li>
            <li><m>L_3^{\prime \prime}(0)=f^{\prime \prime}(0)</m></li>
            <li><m>L_3^{\prime \prime \prime }(0)=f^{\prime \prime \prime}(0)</m></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>L_3(x)=1+x+\frac{1}{2}x^2+\frac{1}{6}x^3</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate your three approximations at <m>x=0.1</m>.
        How do they compare to <m>e^{0.1}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a degree <m>n</m> approximation <m>L_n(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle L_n(x)=\sum _{k=0} ^n \frac{x^k}{k!}</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Plot your degree <m>n</m> approximation in Desmos alongside the function <m>f(x)=e^x</m>.
         Add a slider and observe what happens as <m>n</m> increases? 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <definition xml:id="def-Taylor-polynomial">
    <statement>
      <p>
          Let <m>f(x)</m> be <m>n</m>-times differentiable at <m>x=c</m>.
          The <term>Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=c</m></term>
          is the polynomial <m>\displaystyle T_n(x)=\sum _{k=0} ^n \dfrac{f^{(k)}(c)(x-c)^k}{k!}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <observation>
    <p>By construction, if <m>T_n(x)</m> is the degree <m>n</m>
      Taylor polynomial for <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=c</m>,
      then <m>T_n ^{(i)}(c)=f^{(i)}(c)</m> for all <m>0 \leq i \leq n</m>.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <definition xml:id="def-power-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
         A power series is a function of the form 
         <md>P(x)=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n</md>
         for some real numbers <m>c</m> and <m>a_n</m>.
         That is, <m>P(x)</m> is defined to be the value that 
         the series converges to where it converges, and is undefined
         elsewhere.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the power series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty \frac{x^n}{n!}</m>
        converges everywhere, i.e. for every value of <m>x</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We apply the ratio test.  Compute
        <md> \lim _{n\rightarrow \infty} \left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right| = \frac{n!}{(n+1)!}\left|\frac{x^{n+1}}{x^n}\right| =
        |x| \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{n+1}=0</md>
        Since this limit is <m>0 \lt 1</m> for all values of <m>x</m>, this series converges for all <m>x</m> 
        (i.e. it converges everywhere).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine where the power series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty (-1)^nx^n</m>
        converges.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We apply the ratio test.  Compute
        <md> \lim _{n\rightarrow \infty} \left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right| = |x| </md>
        Thus by the ratio test we know the series converges when <m>|x|\lt 1</m> and diverges 
        when <m>|x| \gt 1</m>.  Since the series <m>\sum _{n=0} ^\infty (-1)^n(1)^n</m> diverges,
        and the series <m>\sum _{n=0} ^\infty (-1)^n(-1)^n</m> also diverges, the power series
        converges precisely on the interval <m>(-1,1)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine where the power series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty 3^n(x-1)^n</m>
        converges.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We apply the ratio test.  Compute
        <md> \lim _{n\rightarrow \infty} \left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right| = \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{3^{n+1}}{3^n} \frac{|x-1|^{n+1}}{|x-1|^n}=3|x-1| </md>
        Thus by the ratio test we know the series converges when <m>3|x-1|\lt 1</m>, i.e. on <m>\left(\frac{2}{3},\frac{4}{3}\right)</m> and diverges 
        when <m>3|x-1| \gt 1</m>.  Since the series <m>\sum _{n=0} ^\infty (3)^n(\frac{4}{3}-1)^n</m> diverges,
        and the series <m>\sum _{n=0} ^\infty (3)^n(\frac{2}{3}-1)^n</m> also diverges, the power series
        converges precisely on the interval <m>\left(\frac{2}{3}, \frac{4}{3}\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-radius-convergence">
    <statement>
      <p>
          Let <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n</m> be a power series.
          Then either 
          <ul>
            <li> <p> The series converges only at <m>x=c</m> </p> </li>
            <li> <p> The series converges everywhere </p> </li>
            <li> 
              <p> There exists <m>R</m> such that the series converges absolutely on 
                the open interval <m>(c-R,c+R)</m> and diverges on <m>(-\infty, c-R) \cup (c+R,\infty)</m>.
              </p> 
            </li>
          </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This follows from <xref ref="lem-interval-convergence"/> below.  Let 
        <md>L=\sup \left\{ x\ \middle | \ \sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n \text{ converges} \right\}</md>.
        Note that <m>L \geq c</m>. If <m>L=c</m> the series converges only at <m>c</m>.
        If <m>L=\infty</m>, then <xref ref="lem-interval-convergence"/> guarantees convergence everywhere.
        Finally, assume <m>L=c+R \gt c</m>.  Then by <xref ref="lem-interval-convergence"/> the series converges
        absolutely on the interval <m>(c-R,c+R)</m>.  Moreover, by construction if the series converges
        at some point <m>b \gt c+R</m>, then <m>b \leq L=c+R</m>, a contradiction; and similarly,
        if it converges at some point <m>b \lt c-R</m>, then by <xref ref="lem-interval-convergence"/>
        it converges at a point larger than <m>c+R</m>, producing the same contradiction.
        So the series must diverge on <m>(-\infty,c-R) \cup (c+R,\infty)</m>
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <definition xml:id="def-interval-convergence">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a power series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n</m>, the maximum interval
        on which it converges is called the <term>interval of convergence</term>.  Since by <xref ref="thm-radius-convergence"/>
        this interval is symmetric about <m>c</m> of length <m>2R</m>, <m>R</m> is called the
        <term>radius of convergence</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <lemma xml:id="lem-interval-convergence">
    <statement>
      <p>
          Let <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n</m> be a power series.
          If the series converges at <m>x=r</m>, then it converges absolutely on
          the interval <m>|x-c| \lt |r-c|</m>, i.e. at all points closer to <m>c</m> than <m>r</m> is.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        By making a substitution <m>y=x-c</m>, we may assume without loss of generality that <m>c=0</m>.
        Assume further without loss of generality that <m>r \gt 0</m>, and let <m>-r \lt b \lt r</m> be given.
        By the root test, since <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n r^n</m> converges, we must have <m>|a_nr^n|^\frac{1}{n} \leq 1</m>
        (since if bigger than 1, the series would diverge by the root test).  Then we compute
         <md>
          |a_nb^n|^\frac{1}{n}=b|a_n|^\frac{1}{n} \lt |r||a_n|^\frac{1}{n} \leq 1
        </md>
        Thus we see by the root test that <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n b^n</m> converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>

  <remark>
    <p>Note that <xref ref="thm-radius-convergence"/> provides no information about convergence
      at the endpoints of the interval of convergence. These must each be checked individually.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find the interval of convergence for each of the following power series.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \frac{x^n}{3n}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \frac{(x-2)^n}{\sqrt{n}}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=2} ^\infty \frac{(x+3)^n}{\ln(n)}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>
  
  <p>The following is incredibly useful.</p>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-power-series-diff-integral">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \displaystyle f(x)=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n </m> be a power series that converges
        on <m>(c-R,c+R)</m> for some <m>R</m>. Then
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f^\prime(x)=\sum _{n=1} ^\infty na_n(x-c)^{n-1}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\int f(x)\ dx=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty \frac{a_n}{n+1}(x-c)^{n+1}+C</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        and these series both also converge on <m>(c-R,c+R)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>\displaystyle f(x)=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty \dfrac{x^n}{n!}</m>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>f^\prime(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\displaystyle \int f(x)\ dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>
  

</section>